.TH AURORA-XART 8 2026-11-07 "aurora-xart 0.1.0" "Aurora Linux"
.SH NAME
aurora-xart \- inspect and manage Linux's Secure Enclave and xART state
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B aurora-xart
.RB [ \-\-root
.IR DIR ]
.B status
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B aurora-xart
.RB [ \-\-root
.IR DIR ]
.B list
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B aurora-xart
.RB [ \-\-root
.IR DIR ]
.B reset-touchid
.RB [ \-\-yes ]
.RB [ \-\-force ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The Secure Enclave of an Apple silicon Mac keeps its anti-replay state in
xART, a store on the iBoot system container that every operating system on
the Mac shares.
On Aurora Linux the
.B apple_sep
driver keeps Linux's own Secure Enclave state in
.IR /var/lib :
the Touch ID identity keybag slot, the catacombs, the machine ref-key, and
the xART guard's list of the session records the enclave created while Linux
ran.
.PP
.B aurora-xart
reads that state and the driver's diagnostics, explains in plain words what is
wrong and what to do, and can move the Touch ID state aside to start Touch ID
over.
It is the Linux counterpart of
.B xartutil
on macOS, limited to what Linux can do safely: it never talks to the enclave,
never writes xART and never deletes a file.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B status
Shows the platform and the SEP profile the driver uses, the driver's
diagnostics from
.IR /sys/bus/platform/devices/*.sep/diag ,
the effective xART guard level, the module parameters with where each value
comes from, whether this is a read-only install, the keybag slot and the
guard list.
It then explains any stuck state, for example an earlier keybag create that
never finished, and says what to run.
With no command,
.B aurora-xart
runs
.BR status .
.TP
.B list
Lists the session records on Linux's xART guard list (kind and session id,
never key material), torn and repeated entries, the one-time allowance, the
saved previous values (kind, session id and length only), the keybag slot
with its state and provenance, and which state files, prints and calibration
files exist.
.TP
.B reset-touchid
Starts Touch ID over on a Mac whose keybag is stuck or no longer loads.
It refuses on a read-only install, when the driver is not running, when xART
writes are disabled this boot, and when the keybag works (unless
.BR \-\-force ).
If Touch ID has not been activated this boot, it opens the sensor once
through
.B fprintd-list
first, since only that loads the keybag.
It says what is lost, asks you to type RESET (unless
.BR \-\-yes ),
masks fprintd until the next boot, and moves each user's
.I /var/lib/fprint/<user>/apple-sep
and the Touch ID state files into a new
.IR /var/lib/aurora-sep-touchid-reset-<time>/ ,
the keybag last.
If a move fails, everything already moved is put back.
It keeps the xART guard's files, the Touch ID calibration and the Touch ID
lockout record.
Reboot afterwards: the driver creates a new keybag during boot, and every
finger is enrolled again.
The old keybag, if the enclave has one, stays there unused.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-root " DIR"
Works on the files under
.IR DIR ,
laid out as on a Mac
.RI ( proc/device-tree ,
.IR sys ,
.IR etc ,
.IR var/lib ),
instead of this system.
Paths are printed relative to it, and this system's services are left alone.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Prints the result as JSON for scripts.
.TP
.B \-\-yes
Does not ask for RESET.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Resets a keybag that works.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Prints the version.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Nothing wrong was found, or the command did what it was asked.
.TP
.B 1
.B status
or
.B list
found a problem that needs action.
.TP
.B 2
The command refused, or the command line was wrong.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/lib/aurora-sep-keybag.bin
The identity keybag slot: committed, an unfinished create (intent) or a
refused create.
.TP
.I /var/lib/aurora-sep-xart-owned.bin
The xART guard's list of the session records Linux created.
.TP
.I /var/lib/aurora-sep-xart-allow-once.bin
A one-time allowance for a boot policy change.
.TP
.I /var/lib/aurora-sep-xart-policy-previous.bin
Saved previous values of records a window or allowance let the enclave
rewrite.
.TP
.I /etc/modprobe.d/aurora-sep.conf
A read-only install's settings
.RB ( "xart_writes=0 provision_keybag=0" ).
.SH NOTES
Reading the state files needs root; the diagnostics and the configuration do
not.
.PP
There is no backup or restore: the enclave's state is anti-replay protected,
so saved Linux-side state cannot be put back into use.
The file formats follow aurora-silicon/linux at commit a20af80f9233, as set
out in
.IR /usr/share/doc/aurora-xart/FORMATS.md .
The xART guard and its recovery are described in aurora-sep's XART-SAFETY.md.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fprintd (8),
.BR modprobe.d (5)
